<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C1.S1 — Normalisation of a qubit state</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C1.S1 — Normalisation</p></div><p>A qubit is prepared in the state \(|\psi\rangle = \frac{1}{\sqrt{ {@a@} }}|0\rangle + b\,|1\rangle\) with \(b\) real and positive.</p><p>Determine \(b\) so that \(\langle\psi|\psi\rangle = 1\). Give an exact expression (no decimals).</p><p>\(b=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Normalisation requires \(|a_0|^2+|a_1|^2=1\), i.e. \(\frac{1}{ {@a@} } + b^2 = 1\).</p><p>Hence \(b^2 = 1-\frac{1}{ {@a@} } = \frac{ {@a-1@} }{ {@a@} }\) and, taking the positive root, \(b = {@ta@}\).</p><p>Note that the global phase of \(|\psi\rangle\) is unobservable, but the sign of \(b\) relative to the \(|0\rangle\) amplitude is not — it is a relative phase. Here positivity was imposed by the statement.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>1.0000000</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1000000</penalty>
    <hidden>0</hidden>
    <idnumber></idnumber>
    <stackversion>
      <text>2024010100</text>
    </stackversion>
    <questionvariables>
      <text>a : rand_with_step(3,9,1);
ta : sqrt((a-1)/a);</text>
    </questionvariables>
    <specificfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>[[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </specificfeedback>
    <questionnote format="html">
      <text>a={@a@}, b={@ta@}</text>
    </questionnote>
    <questiondescription format="html">
      <text></text>
    </questiondescription>
