  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C1.S3 — Pauli algebra: commutator and rotation</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C1.S3 — Pauli algebra</p></div><p>(a) The Pauli matrices satisfy \([\sigma_a,\sigma_b]=2i\varepsilon_{abc}\sigma_c\). Writing \([X,Y]=k\,Z\), give the scalar \(k\) (use <code>i</code> for the imaginary unit).</p><p>\(k=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) Using \((\hat n\cdot\vec\sigma)^2=I\), the rotation \(e^{-i\theta(\hat n\cdot\vec\sigma)/2}\) equals \(\alpha(\theta) I - i\beta(\theta)(\hat n\cdot\vec\sigma)\). Give \(\beta(\theta)\) (use <code>theta</code>).</p><p>\(\beta(\theta)=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) Direct computation: \(XY=iZ\) and \(YX=-iZ\), hence \([X,Y]=2iZ\) and \(k=2i\).</p><p>(b) Split the exponential series. Even powers give \(\sum_k \frac{(-1)^k(\theta/2)^{2k}}{(2k)!}I=\cos(\theta/2)I\); odd powers give \(-i\sin(\theta/2)(\hat n\cdot\vec\sigma)\). Therefore \(\beta(\theta)=\sin(\theta/2)\).</p><p>This identity is the algebraic content of the isomorphism \(SU(2)/\{\pm I\}\cong SO(3)\): a \(2\pi\) rotation returns \(-I\), not \(I\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
